  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nSouthwest flow aloft will translate to a more zonal westerly later\ntonight, with ridging becoming apparent on Friday, lasting into \nSaturday as the axis crosses the Ohio Valley. \n\nAbove normal temperatures will continue, although in the wake of the\nfront, readings will be not quite as warm. Highs may only be 7 to 10\ndegrees above normal on Saturday compared to 10 to 15 degrees above \nnormal today and Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nS/w energy moving east on Sunday will bring the next viable chance \nof rainfall to the region. One fly in the ointment is that the Euro \nand Canadian models are much weaker with this feature and keep the \nforecast dry under a building ridge. Even the GFS has this ridge, \nalbeit much less amplified versus the other models showing l/w \nridging in place during this time. GFS even by its own mass fields \nshould not support the precip that it's giving for Monday. \n\nForecast beyond Sunday is muddled with deterministic models showing \nlarge divergence. This divergence adds the probability of rainfall to\nthe forecast from Sunday onward.",
